content.thinking:
dist/site.css (6.2 kB, Tailwind) and dist/main.js built. The Tailwind scan includes Views/**/*.cshtml, so classes I use will be generated — but only classes present at build time. I'm writing views *now*, after the build. If the classes aren't in the built CSS, the styling won't apply. Since Tailwind v4 with @source scanning, if I add classes after the build, they won't be in site.css until a rebuild. I should rebuild after writing views. Or use a large preflight build. Simplest: write the views, then run `npm run build` again (or watch). The build is fast (130ms). So: write views → rebuild frontend.

Now write the component views under `Views/Shared/Components/<Name>/Default.cshtml`:

Hero:
```cshtml
@model Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models.Blocks.HeroModel
<section class="...">
  @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.Heading)) { <h1 ...>@Model.Heading</h1> }
  @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.Intro)) { <p ...>@Model.Intro</p> }
  @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.ImageUrl)) { <img src="@Model.ImageUrl" .../> }
</section>
```

Note: component views don't inherit _ViewImports? Actually Views/Shared/Components/*/Default.cshtml are views under Views/ so they DO inherit _ViewImports.cshtml. That gives @using Umbraco.Extensions etc. and tag helpers. And the compiler sees `@model` directive. Fine.

RichText:
```cshtml
@model RichTextModel
@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.Heading)) { <h2>@Model.Heading</h2> }
@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.Body)) { @Html.Raw(Model.Body) }
```

Wait — the Body comes from `IHtmlEncodedString`; `ToHtmlString()` returns markup. `@Html.Raw(...)` will not re-encode. Since the source is CMS-provided RTE content, Html.Raw is the standard approach (Umbraco's RTE output is trusted content). Good.

Image block:
```cshtml
<figure>
  @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.ImageUrl)) { <img src="@Model.ImageUrl" alt="@Model.Caption" class="..."/> }
  @if (Caption) <figcaption>@Model.Caption</figcaption>
</figure>
```

Quote:
```cshtml
<blockquote>
  <p>@Model.Quote</p>
  @if (Attribution) <footer>— @Model.Attribution</footer>
</blockquote>
```

Let me write all four component views.

Then the four adapters in Views/Partials/blockgrid/Components/:

heroBlock.cshtml:
```cshtml
@using Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models.Blocks
@inherits Umbraco.Cms.Web.Common.Views.UmbracoViewPage<BlockGridItem<HeroBlock>>
@{
    var model = new HeroModel
    {
        Heading = Model.Content.Heading ?? "",
        Intro = Model.Content.Intro,
        ImageUrl = Model.Content.Image?.GetCropUrl(width: 1600),
    };
}
@await Component.InvokeAsync("Hero", new { model })
```

Need `@using Umbraco.Cms.Core.Models.Blocks` for BlockGridItem. And `@inherits UmbracoViewPage<...>`. The `BlockGridItem<HeroBlock>` needs the using. Add `@using Umbraco.Cms.Core.Models.Blocks` at top.

Actually, better to add the shared usings to `_ViewImports.cshtml`: `@using Umbraco.Cms.Core.Models.Blocks` and `@using Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models.Blocks`. Then adapters are clean. I'll add both usings to _ViewImports.

Let me also make sure GetCropUrl works: `Model.Content.Image?.GetCropUrl(width: 1600)` — Image is MediaWithCrops. The friendly extension on MediaWithCrops: `GetCropUrl(this MediaWithCrops image, int? width = null, ...)`. Since we pass width as named param, that's fine.

imageBlock.cshtml:
```cshtml
@{
    var model = new ImageBlockModel
    {
        ImageUrl = Model.Content.Image?.GetCropUrl(width: 1600),
        Caption = Model.Content.Caption,
    };
}
@await Component.InvokeAsync("ImageBlock", new { model })
```

richTextBlock.cshtml:
